'''Hengli Group''' ({{lang-zh|s=恒力集团}}) is a Chinese privately held company based in [[Suzhou]], [[Jiangsu]]. It operates oil refining, petrochemical, polyester materials and textile businesses.<ref name="FortuneProfile2025" /> [[Reuters]] described the company in 2020 as one of the world's largest polyester yarn producers.<ref name="Reuters2020" />

Hengli was founded in 1994 as a small weaving factory by Chen Jianhua and [[Fan Hongwei]].<ref name="Reuters2020" /> In 2025, Hengli Group ranked 81st on the [[Fortune Global 500]] and 22nd on Fortune China's China 500 list, with Fortune China reporting 2024 revenue of US$121.126 billion.<ref name="FortuneProfile2025" /><ref name="FortuneChina2026" />

== History ==

=== Textile origins ===
Hengli traces its origins to the textile industry in Wujiang, Suzhou. In 1994, Chen Jianhua and Fan Hongwei began operating Wujiang Chemical Fiber Weaving Factory, a small weaving business in Shengze, Suzhou, that Xinhua described as having 27 employees at the time.<ref name="Xinhua Fan 2025">{{cite news |title=范红卫：从"一滴油"到"一匹布" 勇当全产业链发展先行者 |trans-title=Fan Hongwei: From "a drop of oil" to "a piece of cloth", a pioneer in full industrial-chain development |url=https://www.news.cn/photo/20250805/a6f8a248ba0a4857816b3e58ef282a83/c.html |work=Xinhua News Agency |date=5 August 2025 |language=zh |access-date=20 May 2026 |archive-date=20 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520162551/https://www.news.cn/photo/20250805/a6f8a248ba0a4857816b3e58ef282a83/c.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In a 2025 interview published by the All-China Federation of Industry and Commerce, Chen said he had purchased the bankrupt town-run Wujiang Chemical Fiber Weaving Factory for RMB 3.69 million and expanded the business by adding equipment and branch factories.<ref name="ACFIC Chen 2025">{{cite web |title=恒力集团有限公司董事长、总裁陈建华：任何成功都没有捷径，唯有实干 |trans-title=Hengli Group chairman and president Chen Jianhua: There is no shortcut to success, only hard work |url=https://www.acfic.org.cn/myjjzs/qyjjy/202505/t20250529_242896.html |publisher=All-China Federation of Industry and Commerce |date=29 May 2025 |language=zh |access-date=20 May 2026 |archive-date=20 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520162554/https://www.acfic.org.cn/myjjzs/qyjjy/202505/t20250529_242896.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Xinhua reported that Hengli Group was formally established in 2003, with Fan Hongwei serving as general manager.<ref name="Xinhua Fan 2025" />

=== Expansion into polyester and petrochemicals ===
The company expanded from weaving into polyester and then further upstream into petrochemical feedstocks. Hengli Petrochemical's annual report describes an integrated business model in which crude oil and related inputs are processed into products including paraxylene, PTA is produced from paraxylene, and PTA and MEG are then used to produce polyester filaments for textile applications.<ref name="Hengli AR 2025">{{cite report |title=Hengli Petrochemical Co., Ltd. 2025 Annual Report |url=https://stockmc.xueqiu.com/202605/600346_20260512_QVZG.pdf |publisher=Hengli Petrochemical Co., Ltd. |year=2026 |pages=41–42 |access-date=20 May 2026}}</ref> Hengli has described this upstream-to-downstream model as a chain extending from crude oil, aromatics and ethylene to PTA, ethylene glycol, polyester, yarns, films and textiles.<ref name="Hengli intro 2026">{{cite web |title=HENGLI GROUP Introduction |url=https://global.hengli.com/article/835 |publisher=Hengli Group |date=27 March 2026 |access-date=20 May 2026 |archive-date=20 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520162549/https://global.hengli.com/article/835 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Dalian Changxing Island complex ===
[[File:Hengli Petrochemical Industrial Park Sentinel-2 2026.jpg|thumb|right|alt=Satellite image of a coastal petrochemical industrial complex with storage tanks, port facilities, roads, and surrounding terrain on Changxing Island, Dalian.|Sentinel-2 satellite image of Hengli Petrochemical Industrial Park on Changxing Island, Dalian, acquired on 30 May 2026.]]

A major stage in Hengli's expansion was its move into large-scale petrochemicals at Dalian Changxing Island. China Daily reported that Hengli partnered with the Dalian municipal government in 2010 to begin construction of a petrochemical industrial park in the Dalian Changxing Island Economic and Technological Development Zone.<ref name="China Daily 2024">{{cite news |title=Hengli helps revitalize Northeast China |url=https://epaper.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202406/25/WS6679f894a3106431fe82ca4e.html |work=China Daily |date=25 June 2024 |access-date=20 May 2026 |archive-date=20 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520162549/https://epaper.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202406/25/WS6679f894a3106431fe82ca4e.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Xinhua later described Hengli's Dalian Changxing Island site as including a large PTA production base and a 20-million-tonne-per-year refining and chemical integration project.<ref name="Xinhua Fan 2025" />

Reuters reported in 2018 that Hengli was preparing to begin testing an $11 billion oil refinery and petrochemical complex on reclaimed land at Changxing Island.<ref name="Reuters Dalian 2018">{{cite news |last1=Chen |first1=Aizhu |last2=Meng |first2=Meng |title=Hengli's high-tech petroleum complex breathes down PetroChina's neck |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/business/henglis-high-tech-petroleum-complex-breathes-down-petrochinas-neck-idUSKCN1LQ18C/ |work=Reuters |date=12 September 2018 |access-date=20 May 2026}}</ref> Reuters said the facility had a crude oil import quota of 20 million tonnes per year, equal to about 400,000 barrels per day, and included ethylene and paraxylene units in addition to refining capacity.<ref name="Reuters Dalian 2018" />

=== Public listing and restructuring ===
Hengli Petrochemical entered the public equity market through a restructuring of Dalian Rubber & Plastics Machinery, an A-share company already listed on the Shanghai Stock Exchange. Hengli Petrochemical's 2024 annual report states that the company was formerly known as Dalian Rubber & Plastics Machinery Co., Ltd., that the predecessor company was listed on the Shanghai Stock Exchange in 2001, and that its name was changed to Hengli Petrochemical Co., Ltd. on 27 May 2016.<ref name="Hengli AR 2024">{{cite report |title=Hengli Petrochemical Co., Ltd. 2024 Annual Report |url=https://stockn.xueqiu.com/SH600346/20250519294199.pdf |publisher=Hengli Petrochemical Co., Ltd. |year=2025 |pages=161–162 |access-date=20 May 2026 |archive-date=20 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520162549/https://stockn.xueqiu.com/SH600346/20250519294199.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> Reuters also reported in May 2016 that Dalian Rubber & Plastics Machinery would change its name to Hengli Petrochemical.<ref name="Reuters name 2016">{{cite news |title=Dalian Rubber & Plastics Machinery to change name to Hengli Petrochemical |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/business/dalian-rubber-plastics-machinery-to-change-name-to-hengli-petrochemical-idUSL3N18K2WN/ |work=Reuters |date=23 May 2016 |access-date=20 May 2026}}</ref>

The listed company's annual report says the China Securities Regulatory Commission approved Dalian Rubber & Plastics Machinery's major asset restructuring in January 2016, including a share transfer to Hengli Group and the acquisition of Jiangsu Hengli Chemical Fiber assets.<ref name="Hengli AR 2024" /> China Securities Journal later described the transaction as a backdoor listing in which Hengli Petrochemical injected assets worth RMB 10.8 billion into Dalian Rubber & Plastics Machinery at the end of 2015.<ref name="CSJ 2022">{{cite news |title=千亿"炼化茅"拟分拆子公司康辉新材上市，借壳同城企业大连热电 |url=https://finance.sina.cn/2022-04-15/detail-imcwipii4368840.d.html |work=China Securities Journal |via=Sina Finance |date=15 April 2022 |language=zh |access-date=20 May 2026 |archive-date=20 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520162550/https://finance.sina.cn/2022-04-15/detail-imcwipii4368840.d.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2018, Hengli Petrochemical carried out a further asset restructuring that included the acquisition of Hengli Petrochemical (Dalian) Refining Co., Ltd.<ref name="Hengli AR 2024" />

===Environmental concerns===

In 2020, Hengli Group's planned coal-to-polyester project drew environmental concern. [[Reuters]] reported that the company planned a US$20 billion project in [[Yulin, Shaanxi]], to convert coal into polyester yarn, and that coal-based chemical plants typically emitted around three times more carbon dioxide and wastewater per unit of production than oil-based chemical plants.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Chen |first=Aizhu |date=29 July 2020 |title=China's Hengli makes bold $20 billion bet to spin coal into fabric |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/business/chinas-hengli-makes-bold-20-billion-bet-to-spin-coal-into-fabric-idUSKCN24U0M9/ |work=Reuters |access-date=18 May 2026}}</ref> ''Ecotextile News'' described the proposed use of coal as likely to alarm apparel companies with science-based emissions targets.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mowbray |first=John |date=29 July 2020 |title=China textile giant to make polyester from coal |url=https://www.ecotextile.com/2020072939872/news/materials-production-news/china-textile-giant-to-make-polyester-from-coal/ |website=Ecotextile News |access-date=18 May 2026 |archive-date=20 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250720115545/https://www.ecotextile.com/2020072939872/news/materials-production-news/china-textile-giant-to-make-polyester-from-coal/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Ownership and control ==

Hengli Group is a privately held company founded by Chen Jianhua and Fan Hongwei, who are married. The group's main listed arm is Hengli Petrochemical Co., Ltd., which is chaired by Fan Hongwei; Chen Jianhua chairs the parent Hengli Group.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Fan Hongwei |url=https://fortune.com/ranking/most-powerful-women-asia/2024/fan-hongwei/ |website=Fortune |access-date=2026-05-18 |archive-date=2026-05-18 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260518194314/https://fortune.com/ranking/most-powerful-women-asia/2024/fan-hongwei/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In Hengli Petrochemical's 2025 annual report, Hengli Group was identified as the listed company's controlling shareholder, with Chen Jianhua named as Hengli Group's legal representative. The report identified Chen Jianhua and Fan Hongwei as Hengli Petrochemical's actual controllers and stated that, through Hengli Group and parties acting in concert, they directly and indirectly held 5,330,873,649 shares, or 75.73%, of Hengli Petrochemical.<ref name="HengliPetroAR2025" />

== U.S. sanctions ==
{{Further|United States sanctions against Iran|United States sanctions against China}}

On April 24, 2026, the U.S. Department of the Treasury's [[Office of Foreign Assets Control]] (OFAC) sanctioned Hengli Petrochemical (Dalian) Refinery Co., Ltd., a subsidiary of [[Hengli Petrochemical]], under [[Executive Order 13902]], which targets persons operating in Iran's petroleum and petrochemical sectors.<ref name="USTreasury2026">{{Cite web |date=2026-04-24 |title=Economic Fury Targets Global Network Fueling Iran's Oil Trade and Shadow Fleet |url=https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0472 |website=U.S. Department of the Treasury |access-date=2026-06-02 |archive-date=2026-05-29 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260529071336/https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0472 |url-status=live }}</ref> The Treasury Department said the refinery was one of Iran's largest customers for crude oil and petroleum products and alleged that it had purchased billions of dollars' worth of Iranian petroleum.<ref name="USTreasury2026" /><ref name="ReutersSanctions2026">{{Cite news |last=Gardner |first=Timothy |date=2026-04-24 |title=US imposes sanctions on Chinese 'teapot' refinery for buying Iranian oil |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/us-imposes-sanctions-chinese-teapot-refinery-buying-iranian-oil-2026-04-24/ |work=Reuters |access-date=2026-06-02}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Kowsmann |first=Patricia |last2=Feng |first2=Rebecca |last3=Seth |first3=Anika Arora |last4=Paris |first4=Costas |date=2026-08-16 |title=This Sprawling Chinese Refinery Is Bankrolling Tehran |url=https://www.wsj.com/world/china/hengli-china-oil-company-iran-c802475a |url-access=subscription |access-date=2026-08-17 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |language=en-US |issn=0099-9660 |archive-date=2026-08-16 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260816120340/https://www.wsj.com/world/china/hengli-china-oil-company-iran-c802475a |url-status=live }}</ref> OFAC also sanctioned about 40 shipping firms and vessels that it said were involved in Iran's oil trade.<ref name="ReutersSanctions2026" />

Under the sanctions, property and interests in property of designated persons that are in the United States or controlled by U.S. persons are blocked, and U.S. persons are generally prohibited from transactions involving them.<ref name="USTreasury2026" /> OFAC also issued Iran-related General License V, which authorized transactions otherwise prohibited by Executive Order 13902 that were ordinarily incident and necessary to the wind down of transactions involving Hengli Petrochemical (Dalian) Refinery or entities in which it held a 50% or greater interest. The authorization applied through 12:01 a.m. eastern daylight time on May 24, 2026, and OFAC later listed the license as expired.<ref name="OFACGLV2026">{{Cite web |date=2026-04-24 |title=Iran General License V: Authorizing the Wind Down of Transactions Involving Hengli Petrochemical (Dalian) Refinery Co., Ltd. |url=https://ofac.treasury.gov/media/935536/download?inline= |website=Office of Foreign Assets Control |access-date=2026-06-02}}</ref><ref name="OFACExpiredGL2026">{{Cite web |title=Archive of Selected Revoked and Expired General Licenses |url=https://ofac.treasury.gov/sanctions-programs-and-country-information/venezuela-related-sanctions/archive-of-selected-revoked-and-expired-general-licenses |website=Office of Foreign Assets Control |access-date=2026-06-02 |archive-date=2026-03-13 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260313204239/https://ofac.treasury.gov/sanctions-programs-and-country-information/venezuela-related-sanctions/archive-of-selected-revoked-and-expired-general-licenses |url-status=live }}</ref> Reuters described the action as a significant escalation of U.S. efforts to curb Iranian oil revenues, noting that Hengli operated a 400,000-barrel-per-day refining complex in Dalian and was the largest Chinese refiner targeted by the United States since it renewed its crackdown on Iranian oil exports in 2019.<ref name="ReutersExplainer2026">{{Cite news |date=2026-04-29 |title=US sanctions on China's Hengli mark escalation in Iran oil crackdown |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/us-sanctions-chinas-hengli-mark-escalation-iran-oil-crackdown-2026-04-29/ |work=Reuters |access-date=2026-06-02}}</ref>

Hengli Petrochemical denied trading with Iran, saying in a stock-exchange filing that it had "never engaged in any trade with Iran" and that its crude oil suppliers had guaranteed that the crude oil supplied did not fall within the scope of U.S. sanctions. The company said the sanctions lacked factual and legal basis and that it would seek to have the restrictions lifted.<ref name="ReutersDenial2026">{{Cite news |date=2026-04-26 |title=China's Hengli Petrochemical denies trade with Iran in response to US sanctions |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/chinas-hengli-petrochemical-denies-trade-with-iran-response-us-sanctions-2026-04-26/ |work=Reuters |access-date=2026-06-02}}</ref> Following the designation, Hengli Petrochemical's shares fell by 10%, while the company said its Dalian refinery and petrochemical facilities were operating normally and that it had sufficient crude inventories for more than three months of processing.<ref name="ReutersShares2026">{{Cite news |date=2026-04-27 |title=Hengli Petrochemical dives after US imposes Iran oil sanctions |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/hengli-petrochemical-dives-after-us-imposes-iran-oil-sanctions-2026-04-27/ |work=Reuters |access-date=2026-06-02}}</ref>

After the sanctions were imposed, Reuters reported that Hengli Group adjusted the ownership structure of its Singapore-based trading arm, Hengli Petrochemical International, reducing Hengli Petrochemical (Dalian) Refinery's ownership from 100% to 5%, with the remaining stake transferred to Dalian Changxing International Trade, a company owned by a local Chinese government entity.<ref name="ReutersSingapore2026">{{Cite news |date=2026-04-28 |title=Chinese refiner Hengli, sanctioned by US, restructures Singapore unit, sources say |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/chinese-refiner-hengli-sanctioned-by-us-restructures-singapore-unit-sources-say-2026-04-28/ |work=Reuters |access-date=2026-06-02}}</ref> In May 2026, China's Ministry of Commerce issued an injunction under China's blocking rules against recognition or compliance with U.S. sanctions on five Chinese refiners, including Hengli Petrochemical (Dalian) Refinery.<ref name="ReutersMOFCOM2026">{{Cite news |date=2026-05-02 |title=China's Commerce Ministry blocks US sanctions against five refineries |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/chinas-commerce-ministry-blocks-us-sanctions-against-five-refineries-2026-05-02/ |work=Reuters |access-date=2026-06-02}}</ref>

Later in May 2026, Reuters reported that Hengli Petrochemical International, the former Singapore trading arm of Hengli Petrochemical (Dalian) Refinery, planned to cease operations and wind down in late May. Reuters, citing industry sources, reported that some staff had been made redundant while others were to be moved to other Hengli group entities that were not under U.S. sanctions.<ref name="ReutersSingaporeClosure2026">{{Cite news |date=2026-05-11 |title=Hengli Petrochemical's former Singapore unit to cease operations, sources say |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/hengli-petrochemicals-former-singapore-unit-cease-operations-sources-say-2026-05-11/ |work=Reuters |access-date=2026-06-02}}</ref> A Reuters follow-up reported further business effects from the sanctions, including the suspension by [[Wanhua Chemical]] of a long-term agreement to buy benzene from Hengli Petrochemical and potential risk to a preliminary 2024 agreement under which [[Saudi Aramco]] had considered taking a 10% stake in Hengli Petrochemical. Reuters also reported that Hengli's largely domestic focus and Beijing's support could allow it to continue operating largely as usual, while traders said it had redirected petrochemical sales to the domestic market.<ref name="ReutersHengliChill2026">{{Cite news |last1=Howe |first1=Colleen |last2=Chen |first2=Aizhu |date=2026-05-22 |title=Hengli, China's silk-to-petrochemicals empire, faces the chill of US sanctions |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/hengli-chinas-silk-to-petrochemicals-empire-faces-chill-us-sanctions-2026-05-22/ |work=Reuters |access-date=2026-06-02}}</ref>

